{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 33, :comments ["<b>And an ox falls there.</b> Rashi explains: the same applies to all other livestock, for in every place where 'ox' is stated, we learn 'ox–ox' from the Sabbath, and 'ox or donkey' is not written except for the midrash: 'ox' and not man, 'donkey' and not utensils. And if you ask: but one of them was needed to learn from the Sabbath, and if so, how would he exclude man and utensils? One answer: had he not excluded man and utensils, he should have written 'and fell,' and nothing more, and it would imply that anything that falls there, whether any livestock, man, or utensils, he would be liable; if so, from 'fell' he includes everything, just as on the Sabbath 'ox and donkey' — why do I need them? Thus you learn that both are needed for the derashah. Yet it is difficult: why do I need 'ox and not man'? Let it emerge from the fact that it is written והמת יהיה לו — 'he whose dead it is' — this excludes one whose dead it is not, for a corpse is forbidden in benefit. And one answer: it speaks of a Canaanite slave of an Israelite who was neither circumcised nor immersed, who is permitted in benefit, as we say in the Yerushalmi. Another difficulty: let it be resolved in a gentile corpse. And the reason a corpse is forbidden in benefit is because we learn it from the eglah arufah, as it is written וערפו שם, and it is written ותמת שם מרים — he requires a parallel to Miriam, who was an Israelite woman, to exclude an idolater."], :book :exodus, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:03:54.628703027Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}